1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,603
Search "Schedule Trigger" and add it to your canvas.

2
00:00:03,603 --> 00:00:08,857
Set the interval to weekly, on Monday, at whatever time you actually want the report

3
00:00:08,857 --> 00:00:09,858
waiting for you.

4
00:00:09,858 --> 00:00:14,988
This is the first trigger in this course that runs on a clock instead of an event,

5
00:00:14,988 --> 00:00:20,242
and it is worth noticing how little configuration that actually takes: one dropdown,

6
00:00:20,242 --> 00:00:21,931
one time, and you are done.

7
00:00:21,931 --> 00:00:26,873
One honest self hosting caveat before you pick the time, because the clock only

8
00:00:26,873 --> 00:00:28,562
ticks while n8n is running.

9
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:33,691
Schedule the report for eight and open the laptop at nine, and Monday's run simply

10
00:00:33,691 --> 00:00:38,758
did not happen: there is no little machine in the cloud owed to you, that was the

11
00:00:38,758 --> 00:00:40,197
trade from Section 2.1.

12
00:00:40,197 --> 00:00:45,201
So pick a time that matches your real Monday, half past nine if that is when the

13
00:00:45,201 --> 00:00:50,393
machine is reliably open, and the schedule becomes dependable by design rather than

14
00:00:50,393 --> 00:00:50,894
by luck.

15
00:00:50,894 --> 00:00:55,961
The day your team relies on this report existing before anyone logs on is the day

16
00:00:55,961 --> 00:00:58,463
the hosted option earns its monthly fee.

17
00:00:58,463 --> 00:01:02,717
Until then, schedule around your actual habits, not your ideal ones.

18
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:06,439
Why does the trigger menu matter enough for a table?

19
00:01:06,439 --> 00:01:11,014
Because choosing the wrong "when" is the commonest design error in beginner

20
00:01:11,014 --> 00:01:13,759
workflows, and it is invisible on the canvas.

21
00:01:13,759 --> 00:01:18,821
A report on an email trigger runs at the mercy of whoever emails; a triage agent on

22
00:01:18,821 --> 00:01:21,200
a schedule reads mail in stale batches.

23
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:26,019
Match the trigger to the shape of the work: reactive, handed over, or rhythmic.

24
00:01:26,019 --> 00:01:31,082
You now hold all three, and every workflow you ever sketch starts by picking one of

25
00:01:31,082 --> 00:01:32,119
these three rows.

26
00:01:32,119 --> 00:01:36,999
While you are in the schedule panel, notice the faster cadences on offer, daily,

27
00:01:36,999 --> 00:01:41,879
hourly, custom, and resist them for this build: a report that arrives more often

28
00:01:41,879 --> 00:01:46,759
than anyone reads it trains its audience to ignore it, and weekly is part of the

29
00:01:46,759 --> 00:01:47,186
design.

30
00:01:47,736 --> 00:01:50,549
Look at the pinned output on the right of that panel.

31
00:01:50,549 --> 00:01:53,892
Five rows, four columns: Client, Premium, Renewal Date, Status.

32
00:01:53,892 --> 00:01:58,244
That is the entire shape of data your agent has to reason about, and it is exactly

33
00:01:58,244 --> 00:02:01,587
the shape you will point it at once you connect your own sheet.

34
00:02:01,587 --> 00:02:06,045
Those column headers deserve more respect than headers usually get, because they are

35
00:02:06,045 --> 00:02:06,947
now an interface.

36
00:02:06,947 --> 00:02:11,299
The rows arrive on the wire with each value filed under its header's name, and the

37
00:02:11,299 --> 00:02:15,333
next lecture's prompt will refer to Premium and Status by those exact words.

38
00:02:15,333 --> 00:02:19,684
Which means the sheet's header row has quietly become part of your agent: rename a

39
00:02:19,684 --> 00:02:23,824
column to tidy things up, and the agent goes on reading a field that no longer

40
00:02:23,824 --> 00:02:24,195
exists.

41
00:02:24,195 --> 00:02:28,494
If the sheet belongs to more than one person, that is worth a team agreement now,

42
00:02:28,494 --> 00:02:31,784
headers do not change without telling whoever runs the report.

43
00:02:31,784 --> 00:02:33,270
File that thought carefully.

44
00:02:33,270 --> 00:02:37,728
Section 7 is built around the day a shared sheet changes shape in a subtler way than

45
00:02:37,728 --> 00:02:42,133
a rename. [SCREENSHOT-NEEDED: your own Google account connection screen for Sheets,

46
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:46,113
since that page belongs to your account and cannot be captured generically]

47
00:02:46,663 --> 00:02:51,301
It is easy to skim past a dropdown when you are moving fast, and this is the one

48
00:02:51,301 --> 00:02:54,490
dropdown in this build actually worth slowing down for.

49
00:02:54,490 --> 00:02:59,128
Get it wrong, and read only stops being true no matter what your job description

50
00:02:59,128 --> 00:02:59,940
says on paper.

51
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:04,751
The general principle is worth extracting, because it recurs in every tool you will

52
00:03:04,751 --> 00:03:08,520
ever connect: capability and permission live in different places.

53
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:13,274
The node is capable of writing, always was, and no setting removes that capability

54
00:03:13,274 --> 00:03:14,317
from the software.

55
00:03:14,317 --> 00:03:19,187
What the dropdown holds is your refusal to use it, which makes it the piece of paper

56
00:03:19,187 --> 00:03:21,912
where this agent's action level actually lives.

57
00:03:21,912 --> 00:03:26,782
So give it the same treatment as any load bearing setting: glance at it whenever you

58
00:03:26,782 --> 00:03:31,536
edit this workflow for any reason, because future you, adding a column or tweaking

59
00:03:31,536 --> 00:03:36,406
the range, is one careless dropdown away from an agent that writes to the very sheet

60
00:03:36,406 --> 00:03:37,217
it reports on.

61
00:03:37,767 --> 00:03:40,347
That third item is not decoration either.

62
00:03:40,347 --> 00:03:45,507
Your pinned test rows need at least one renewal sitting close to its date, because

63
00:03:45,507 --> 00:03:50,730
the risk list is the report's whole reason to exist, and test data where nothing is

64
00:03:50,730 --> 00:03:55,765
at risk proves only that the agent can say "all fine", which is the one sentence

65
00:03:55,765 --> 00:03:56,960
nobody ever checks.

66
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:58,974
Give the test something to find.

67
00:03:58,974 --> 00:04:04,197
The pinned set this course uses does exactly that: five clients, four open, and one

68
00:04:04,197 --> 00:04:09,483
of them close enough to its renewal date that a good report has no choice but to say

69
00:04:09,483 --> 00:04:09,671
so.

70
00:04:09,671 --> 00:04:14,894
Next lecture is where those five rows stop being a table and become three sentences

71
00:04:14,894 --> 00:04:18,167
you would actually want to read on a Monday morning.
